# Single source of truth — consts & enums

Reusable strings, numbers, roles, statuses, and codes belong in a **constants file**, not inline in routes, services, or components. The frontend **imports** those files; it does not re-hardcode the values.

## Location & naming

| App | Folder | File name |
|-----|--------|-----------|
| API | `apps/api/src/constants/` | `<domain>.constant.ts` |
| Web | `apps/web/constants/` | `<domain>.constant.ts` |

Examples: `org.constant.ts`, `issue.constant.ts`. Not `org.ts`, not `org.constants.ts`, and not inside `lib/`, `services/`, or a component.

```ts
// ✅ GOOD — apps/web/constants/org.constant.ts
export const OrgRole = { ADMIN: 'admin', EMPLOYEE: 'employee' } as const;
export type OrgRoleValue = (typeof OrgRole)[keyof typeof OrgRole];
```

```ts
// ✅ GOOD — import on the client
import { OrgRole } from '@/constants/org.constant';
if (member.role === OrgRole.ADMIN) { /* … */ }
```

```ts
// ❌ BAD — magic string in a component / service
if (member.role === 'admin') { /* … */ }
```

Prefer `as const` objects (this repo’s default). Use `enum` only when it clearly fits.

## Don't overdo

- One-off UI copy, log messages, or a value used once → leave inline
- Don't invent enums for every prop or boolean
- Until `packages/shared` exists, domain values used on both sides (roles, issue status) are mirrored in **both** constant folders with the **same keys and values** — don't invent a second spelling
- New files use `*.constant.ts`; rename older API files (`org.ts`, `brand.constants.ts`) when you next touch them

## Agent habit

Before hardcoding a role, status, error code, cookie name, or path segment: add or reuse `*.constant.ts`, then import it (including on the web client).
